// The `log` format, v1, which reads v0 (the versions are `log.rs`'s
// `READS`; what arrived in v1 is scoped there, not here). This file is NORMATIVE: the Rust reader in
// diet/src/formats/log.rs implements it and is not itself the definition.
//
// A session's event log (#117, ruled 2026-09-26): what a drive did, in the
// order it did it, one event per line. Two things read it -- the record
// projection and the surface, `exercise` -- and that is what makes it a format
// rather than a drive-internal detail. The record stays the durable artifact
// derived from it.
//
// THE VALUE TERMINALS ARE THE RECORD'S. `object`, `string`, `decimal`,
// `integer`, `boolean` come from `record/grammar.pest` (and `number.pest`),
// which the reader concatenates ahead of this file, and every value is read
// by the record's one value reader (`record::json::line`). So there is no
// `null` and no binary float here either, for the record's reasons: absence
// is an omitted key, and a number reads back as the digits written.
//
// What this file adds is the document:
//
//   * ONE EVENT PER LINE, AND NOTHING BETWEEN THEM. No blank line, no
//     indentation, `\n` only. The sequence number is the primary key and it
//     is gapless; a blank line would be a line with no key.
//
//   * A LAST LINE MAY END THE TEXT WITHOUT A NEWLINE, because a log being
//     written is read while it is open.
//
// Not said here, and checked in Rust under a name, as the record does with
// its own cross-row rules: what each `kind` carries, and the rules that span
// rows -- the first line is `session.start` and carries the version; `seq`
// counts from 0 without a gap; `t` never decreases; a `settlement` leaves
// the state the previous one entered; turns count from 1 by one; a reference
// names an earlier `request` by its `seq`. A back-reference across lines is
// not what a PEG is for.

log_document = { SOI ~ log_line+ ~ EOI }

log_line = { object ~ ("\n" | &EOI) }
